26
00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:18.300
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And I think what I will mainly do here is use this diagram to talk through what the PR has added.

27
00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:23.019
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So this picture shows… shows the number of piling nodes.

28
00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:31.829
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): all participating in a Paladin domain token, a notarized token.

29
00:10:31.880 --> 00:10:44.879
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): specifically showing where, where, a noto transaction, only really involves two of the four notes. So, it involves the note that's the notary, and it involves the note that submitted it.

30
00:10:45.830 --> 00:10:59.320
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And this diagram, once it's finalized, will go into the docs, and it shows the information that gets persisted at a node, the information that goes on chain, and the information that gets sent to other nodes.

31
00:10:59.960 --> 00:11:07.290
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So the intent of this is to show that if a user submits a transaction to a node.

32
00:11:07.670 --> 00:11:23.390
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): That will have some coordination between the originating node, and some of the terminology that we're using for the new sequencing is important here. So, we have an originating node, which is where the transaction originated, that's node 1.

33
00:11:23.860 --> 00:11:30.149
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And in this case, we have a coordinating node at node 2, so there'll always be a coordinating node, but it won't always be a different node.

34
00:11:30.340 --> 00:11:45.990
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): You have domains that self-coordinate, and in this case, we have a domain which is always being coordinated by node 2, and so you have the originator on the left, the coordinator node 2, and the other two nodes are not doing anything with this transaction, even though they're participating in this domain.

35
00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:48.370
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): What's domain instance, to be fair.

36
00:11:49.350 --> 00:12:08.409
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So, the notary coordinates everything, but as it does that coordinating, there's quite a lot of complexity to what it does, and it might go through several iterations. In this case, it attempts three submissions to the base ledger, two of which fail, they revert for some reason, and then the third one is successful. And while all this is going on.

37
00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:16.079
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): The user who submitted their transaction, it can be very hard to determine, while it's stuck in pending, what's happened with it.

38
00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:30.879
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): They don't necessarily know that there are… there are reverting transactions on the base ledger that are causing it to be delayed. They don't know that the node, the notary node has actually done anything with it, necessarily. It might have sent an assemble request to node 1, but then it might have gone quiet.

39
00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:36.930
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And it might be assembling other things as well. So, the intent of this is to help understand what's happening.

40
00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:53.229
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And to help distribute information about the base ledger transactions. So, what we see here is that the notary coordinating this transaction sends… every time it submits a public transaction, it sends out a submission with the full public transaction.

41
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:58.569
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): back to the originating node. So we can see these grey lines here going back to the originating node.

42
00:12:58.920 --> 00:13:04.220
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And that means both for the failure cases and the successful cases,

43
00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:23.449
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): The, user at node 1 can query their transaction, and they can get a list of the public transaction hashes that have been involved in attempting to get their transaction, endorsed and accepted on the base ledger contract.

44
00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:35.600
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And so they can see what's been happening, and they can see if any public transactions have been attempted, and they can see the outcome of them by looking at the base ledger for those transaction hashes.

45
00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:46.529
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): What we also have, though, is other activity that might happen before we even attempt to put things on the base ledger, and we're calling that a sequencing activity.

46
00:13:46.530 --> 00:14:00.870
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So the new sequencer, is a distributed sequencer, it's designed to be able to be distributed across different nodes, in the future, and we term… anything that it's doing is sequencing activity, and that activity,

47
00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:14.299
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): involves several steps. Anna will talk briefly about the state machine, in a minute, and as the state machine goes through the different states for transactions, all of those things are what we call sequencing activity.

48
00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:21.850
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And what this PR starts to do is to put some of that activity… we're just starting with a minimal

49
00:14:22.050 --> 00:14:27.389
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): distribution to begin with, but this PR introduces that mechanism.

50
00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:46.379
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): is that the coordinator, while it is involved in sequencing a transaction, it will send some of that information back to the originator. So we get sequencing activity, and that means in the future, we might have a node that's going through several state changes for a transaction, and hasn't actually attempted a public transaction.

51
00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:52.029
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So that the originator wouldn't have public transactions to look at, but they would see that sequencing activity.

52
00:14:52.410 --> 00:15:12.390
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): So they can see that sequencing is going on, they can look at that, they can understand that the coordinator's trying to, progress it, and really, really usefully, they can see who that coordinator is. So they can see that, it's node 2 is doing stuff, but I could go and talk to the administrator of node 2, or the organization running it. I've got IDs that I can correlate with them, and so we can understand what's going on.

53
00:15:12.610 --> 00:15:20.129
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And in the future, the coordinator for this notarized token might, might, might fail over to another node.

54
00:15:20.530 --> 00:15:33.610
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And so, being able to see that the dispatcher, for example, for a dispatch to the base ledger has now changed to another node, all of this is useful information in terms of diagnostics and monitoring and observability.

55
00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:43.419
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): And then we've extended PTX getTransactionFull and PTX getTransactionReceiptFull to return, those bits of information.

56
00:15:43.640 --> 00:16:00.719
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): They could be quite verbose, so the regular PTX get transaction, the non-full variants, don't return all of this information, because they could get quite large. But we're starting to make that available by telling the relevant nodes. You can see node 2 doesn't tell any of this to nodes 3 and 4.

57
00:16:00.780 --> 00:16:08.060
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): is not relevant to Notes 3 and 4. Privacy of the activity is, is, you know, always,

58
00:16:08.430 --> 00:16:16.199
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): foremost in the design of the way Palladium works. So while there might be other transactions that use the same NOTO domain instance.

59
00:16:16.200 --> 00:16:29.969
Matthew Whitehead (Kaleido): This transaction, nothing is told in those 3 and 4 about public baseload transactions, or the sequencing activity, and that's a really important part of the design, is that only the relevant parties are given information about what's taking place.

